🛡️ Incident Response Fundamentals

Platform: TryHackMe

Difficulty: Beginner → Intermediate
Time: ~60 mins
Focus Area: Cybersecurity | Incident Response | EDR Analysis

🧩 Overview

This project documents my practical work from the TryHackMe - Incident Response Fundamentals room.
The lab introduces essential concepts and real-world practices of Incident Response (IR) — identifying, analyzing, containing, eradicating, and recovering from cybersecurity incidents.

📚 Learning Objectives

Understand what constitutes a security incident

Differentiate between types of incidents (malware, phishing, insider threats, etc.)

Learn the Incident Response Process (Preparation → Identification → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned)

Apply Incident Response techniques in a simulated environment

Use an Endpoint Detection & Response (EDR) tool to investigate and respond to malware infections

⚙️ Lab Scenario

Scenario Summary:
A phishing email containing a malicious attachment was downloaded by multiple employees. The goal is to investigate the spread of the malware across hosts and take appropriate actions.

Tasks Performed:

Identify affected hosts

Checked which devices executed or downloaded the malicious file.

Analyze EDR Dashboard Data

Verified host states (Executed / Not Executed).

Located impacted endpoints (Host-ATYU, Host-IOPE, Host-HKNV).

Take Response Actions

Quarantine files on hosts where malware was not executed (Host-ATYU, Host-IOPE).

Investigate executed hosts (Host-HKNV) to trace infection timeline.

Document Findings & Timeline

Recorded infection spread and actions taken for each host.

🧠 Key Takeaways

Importance of rapid response to phishing-based intrusions.

How EDR tools assist in detecting and isolating infected systems.

Hands-on understanding of containment and remediation strategies.

Reinforcement of incident documentation and evidence preservation.

🖼️ Evidence


The EDR environment displaying host infection states and response actions.

